// The traits threads are checked with. Like `Dull` and `Light`, the
// compiler implements them from a type's parts:
//
// * `Send`: a value may move to another thread. Everything is Send except
//   raw pointers, mutable slices (`[T; &mut]`, which are copied freely, so
//   two threads could write through the same one), `FmtArgs`, shared slices
//   of items that are not Sync, closures that change a variable they
//   borrow, and types holding one of those.
// * `Sync`: threads may share views of a value. Everything is Sync except
//   what is not Send and `core::cell::UnsafeCell` (whose value changes
//   behind shared views), and types holding one of those.
// * `Detached`: the value holds no views (no `str`, slice or closure that
//   borrows), so nothing in it points into data it does not own and it may
//   outlive every local variable. Owners, Dull values and raw pointers are
//   Detached. A thread started by `std::thread::spawn` may run longer than
//   the function that started it, so it takes only Detached values.
//
// A struct or enum may promise `Send` or `Sync` with `unsafe impl` (when it
// holds raw pointers or cells and guards them itself, like
// `std::sync::Mutex`), or take them away with a negative impl. Its impls
// then decide alone; its fields no longer count. `Detached` cannot be
// implemented by hand.

#[lang = "send"]
pub trait Send {}

#[lang = "sync"]
pub trait Sync {}

#[lang = "detached"]
pub trait Detached {}
